Subject: [PATCH] arm64 kernel: don't free a page table a user map never had

A user VMSAv8TranslationMap is created with fPageTable 0 and gets its
top-level table on the first Map(). One destroyed before anything was
mapped -- fork_team() giving up for lack of memory, after creating the
child's address space -- has no table, but the destructor called
FreeTable(0, ...) regardless. That walks physical page 0 through the
kernel's physical map (0xffffff0000000000), which is not RAM on QEMU's
virt machine, and the first atomic swap faults:

    PANIC: vm_page_fault: unhandled page fault in kernel space at
           0xffffff0000000000, ip ... __aarch64_swp8_acq_rel
    VMSAv8TranslationMap::~VMSAv8TranslationMap
    VMAddressSpace::~VMAddressSpace
    fork_team
    _kern_fork  (sshd-session; R Chromium's content_shell)

Seen twice on the RENKU arm64 VM (4 GB) after "low resource memory:
critical": a failed fork, which should have been an error the caller
handles, took the machine into the kernel debugger instead.

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
index c4ca593238..b7d74724c3 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
@@ -151,9 +151,14 @@ VMSAv8TranslationMap::~VMSAv8TranslationMap()
 		}
 	}
 
-	vm_page_reservation reservation = {};
-	FreeTable(fPageTable, 0, fInitialLevel, &reservation);
-	vm_page_unreserve_pages(&reservation);
+	// A user map gets its top-level table on the first Map() call, so one
+	// destroyed before anything was mapped -- fork_team() failing for lack
+	// of memory -- has none, and FreeTable(0) would walk physical page 0.
+	if (fPageTable != 0) {
+		vm_page_reservation reservation = {};
+		FreeTable(fPageTable, 0, fInitialLevel, &reservation);
+		vm_page_unreserve_pages(&reservation);
+	}
 }
 
 
